#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Rational Exponents Recap 📝]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Rational exponents represent roots 🌱]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- $a^(m/n) = root(n, a^m)$ ✅]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Simplify, multiply, divide, solve equations 🧮]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("In this chapter, we learned that rational exponents are another way to represent roots.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("The expression a to the power of m over n is equal to the nth root of a to the power of m.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("We can simplify expressions with rational exponents, multiply and divide them, and solve equations involving them using specific rules.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Simplifying Rational Exponents 🔍]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Convert between rational exponent and radical form]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Example: $8^(1/3) = 2$ 🎯]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("To simplify expressions with rational exponents, we can convert them to radical form and vice versa.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("For example, 8 to the power of one-third simplifies to 2, because the cube root of 8 is 2.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Multiplying and Dividing 🧮]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Multiplying: $a^(m/n) a^(p/q) = a^((m/n) + (p/q))$]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Dividing: $a^(m/n) / a^(p/q) = a^((m/n) - (p/q))$]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("When multiplying expressions with rational exponents, we add the exponents and simplify.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("When dividing, we subtract the exponents and simplify.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Solving Equations 🔢]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Isolate the term with the exponent]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Raise both sides to the reciprocal of the exponent]
#v(20pt)
#only("3-")[- Example: $x^(1/2) = 4 arrow x = 16$ 🎉]
#only("1")[
#voiceover("To solve equations with rational exponents, we first isolate the term with the exponent.")
]
#only("2")[
#voiceover("Then, we raise both sides of the equation to the reciprocal of the exponent.")
]
#only("3")[
#voiceover("For example, if x to the power of one-half equals 4, then x equals 16, because the square of 4 is 16.")
]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Key Takeaways 🔑]
#v(40pt)
